<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-binomial-DRV" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Bernoulli and Binomial Random Variables</title>

 <introduction>
 <p> 
    By completing the problems provided below, you will gain experience using the Bernoulli and Binomial random variables to answer questions involving random outcomes and probabilities. You will practice:     
    <ul>
        <li>
        <p>
          Identifying when the Bernoulli and Binomial distributions can be used to model a scenario. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Using the Binomial probability distribution function to compute probabilities.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Constructing the cumulative distributions functions for a Binomial random variable.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Using the expected value and variance formulas for a Binomial random variable.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>


 <exercises xml:id="hw-binom-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>


   <exercise xml:id="ex-binomial-approximation-scenarios" interactive="multiple-choice">
    <title>Is this Binomial? [Part 1]</title>
    <statement>
       <p>
            In a recent year, the nationwide average score on the National Academic Assessment (NAA) was 1050. Suppose that exactly 54% of all high school graduates took this exam, and that female students comprise 51% of all high school graduates nationwide. 
        </p>

        <p>
            A random sample of 120 high school graduates is selected from across the country.  Below you are presented with multiple random variables related to this scenario.  Select all that can be be considered Binomial/can be modeled with a Binomial distribution.
        </p>

    </statement>
   <hint>
        <p>
            To determine if a variable fits the binomial model, check if it satisfies the following assumptions:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Binary - Are there exactly two complementary outcomes for each trial (e.g., Success vs. Failure)? Bernoulli trials!</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p> Independent - Does the outcome of one trial have no effect on the outcome of another?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p> Fixed Number of Trials - Is the sample size (<m>n</m>) established before counting begins?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p> Constant Probability of Success - Does the probability (<m>p</m>) stay constant for every single trial?</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
      
    </hint>


    <choices multiple="yes" randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
           <statement>
                     <p> The number of students in the sample who took the NAA.</p>
                </statement>

                <feedback>
                <p>Correct! This counts independent "successes" (taking the test) out of a fixed number of trials. Here, <m>n = 120</m> and <m>p = 0.54</m>.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
                <p>The individual exam scores of the 120 students in the sample.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect. These are numerical values measured on a continuous scale, not a count of binary successes/failures.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>The number of students in the sample who scored above average on the NAA.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! Each student either scored above average or they did not (binary outcomes). While <m>n = 120</m>, the exact value of <m>p</m> is  unknown based on the given information.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
                <p>The amount of time required by each student in the sample to complete the NAA.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect. Time is a continuous quantitative random variable, not a discrete count of binary events.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement>
                <p>The number of female high school graduates in the sample.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! This is a classic binomial counting scenario with a fixed sample size. Each student is either female or not (binary outcomes). Here, <m>n = 120</m> and <m>p = 0.51</m>.</p>
            </feedback>

        </choice>
    </choices>
</exercise>




<exercise xml:id="ex-identifying-discrete-distributions" interactive="multiple-choice">
    <title>Is this Binomial? [Part 2]</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An electronics logistics warehouse receives a batch of 80 smart sensors, exactly 8 of which have a calibration defect. A quality control engineer plans to audit the shipment under various testing protocols. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Select all of the following random variables that can be modeled using a Binomial distribution (or are well-approximated by one).
        </p>
    </statement>
   
    <choices multiple="yes" randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>The engineer randomly selects 5 sensors with replacement (testing and returning each one before picking the next), and records the number of defective sensors found.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! Because sampling is done with replacement, the trials are independent and the probability of finding a defect stays perfectly constant at <m>p = 8/80 = 0.10</m> across a fixed <m>n = 5</m> trials. </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
                <p>The engineer randomly selects 5 sensors without replacement from the batch of 80, and records the number of defective sensors found.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect. Because the batch is small (<m>N = 80</m>) and sampling is done without replacement, the trials are dependent—the probability of a defect changes with each pull. This describes a Hypergeometric distribution, not a Binomial one.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
                <p>The engineer tests sensors one by one with replacement and records the total number of sensors they must test until they discover their very first defective unit.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect. This scenario counts the number of trials required to achieve the first success, which defines a Geometric distribution, not a Binomial one.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <p>The warehouse receives a massive new fleet of 15,000 components from an assembly line where 2% are known to be defective. The engineer randomly selects 50 components without replacement and counts the defective units.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Correct! While sampling is technically without replacement, the sample size (<m>n = 50</m>) is less than 10% of the massive population (<m>N = 15,000</m>). The independence assumption holds, making the Binomial approximation to the hypergeometric perfectly appropriate here (<m>n=50, p=0.02</m>).</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
                <p>The engineer randomly selects 5 sensors with replacement and records the total weight of the selected units.</p>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect. Total weight is a continuous quantitative metric, not a discrete count of binary success events.</p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
</exercise>


<exercise label="ex-binomial-colorado-energy-realistic" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Colorado Energy Conservation</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Many utility companies promote environmental sustainability and energy conservation by offering discount rates to consumers who keep their monthly energy usage below certain levels. A recent state environmental report notes that 35% of residential energy consumers in Colorado have reduced their home electricity usage sufficiently to qualify for these discounted rates. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose an inspector randomly selects a sample of 5 residential energy consumers from Denver. Let <m>X</m> denote the number of consumers who qualify for the discounted rates. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability Calculation (At least 4) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-prob-at-least-4">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that at least four of the five randomly selected residential consumers qualify for the discounted energy rates. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.054" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                    <p> Correct! <m>X \sim \text{Binomial}(n = 5, p = 0.35)</m>, and
                    <me>P(X \ge 4) = P(X = 4) + P(X = 5) = 0.04877 + 0.00525 = 0.05402,</me>

where           <me>P(X = 4) = \binom{5}{4} (0.35)^4 (0.65)^1,</me> and       <me>P(X = 5) = \binom{5}{5} (0.35)^5 (0.65)^0 </me>.  </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                Because we are drawing a fixed sample of independent residential households (<m>n = 5</m>) where each household has a constant, binary success probability of qualifying (<m>p = 0.35</m>), the random variable <m>X</m> follows a standard Binomial distribution: <m>X \sim \text{Binomial}(n = 5, p = 0.35)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The query asks for the probability that <term>at least four</term> households qualify, which corresponds to the sum of the disjoint discrete point probabilities for exactly 4 and exactly 5 successes:
                <me>P(X \ge 4) = P(X = 4) + P(X = 5)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating each point using the standard Binomial probability mass function formula, <m>P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}</m>:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 4 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 4) = \binom{5}{4} (0.35)^4 (0.65)^1 = 5 \cdot 0.015006 \cdot 0.65 \approx 0.04877</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 5 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 5) = \binom{5}{5} (0.35)^5 (0.65)^0 = 1 \cdot 0.005252 \cdot 1 \approx 0.00525</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Summing these individual probabilities together yields the cumulative interval area:
                <me>P(X \ge 4) = 0.04877 + 0.00525 = 0.05402</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that at least four households qualify for the favorable rates is exactly <strong>0.0540</strong> (or a 5.40% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Second Probability Computation Task (Fewer than 2) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-prob-fewer-than-2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that fewer than two of the five randomly selected residential consumers qualify for the discounted energy rates. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin answer="0.428" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                    <p> Correct! <m>X \sim \text{Binomial}(n = 5, p = 0.35)</m>, and
                    <me>P(X \lt 2) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) = 0.116029+0.312386 \approx 0.428,</me>

where           <me>P(X = 0) = \binom{5}{0} (0.35)^0 (0.65)^5,</me> and       
<me>P(X = 1) = \binom{5}{1} (0.35)^1 (0.65)^4 </me>.  </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                The query asks for the probability that <term>fewer than two</term> households qualify, which corresponds to the sum of the disjoint discrete point probabilities for exactly 0 and exactly 1 success:
                <me>P(X \lt 2) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating each point using the Binomial probability mass function formula:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 0 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 0) = \binom{5}{0} (0.35)^0 (0.65)^5 = 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 0.11603 \approx 0.11603</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 1 Qualifies:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 1) = \binom{5}{1} (0.35)^1 (0.65)^4 = 5 \cdot 0.35 \cdot 0.17851 \approx 0.31239</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Summing these individual probabilities together yields the cumulative interval area:
                <me>P(X \lt 2) = 0.11603 + 0.31239 = 0.42842</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that fewer than two households qualify for the favorable rates is exactly <strong>0.4284</strong> (or a 42.84% chance).
            </p>
        </solution> -->
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Expected Value and Variance -->
        <!-- Part C: Expected Value and Variance (Converted to Multiple Choice with Interpretation) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-moments-mc">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the expected value and the standard deviation for the number of qualifying residential consumers in the random sample of  <m>5</m>. Which option correctly identifies these values and delivers the most accurate  interpretation of their meaning?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p> We expect nearly 2 residential consumers (<m>E(X) = 1.75</m>) to qualify, though this could vary by 1.07 residential consumers.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! <m>E(X) = n \cdot p = 5 \cdot 0.35 = 1.75</m>  and <m>V(X) = n \cdot p \cdot (1 - p) = 5 \cdot 0.35 \cdot 0.65 = 1.1375 \approx 1.14</m>. Then <m>\sqrt{V(X)}=1.07</m></p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>  We expect 3-4 residential consumers (<m>E(X) = 3.25</m>) to qualify, give or take 1.07 residential consumers.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This configuration uses a success probability of <m>p = 0.65</m> rather than the correct <m>p = 0.35</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice >
                <statement>
                     <p>  We expect 1-2 residential consumers (<m>E(X) = 1.75</m>) to qualify, give or take 1.14 residential consumers.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This answer uses the variance, rather than standard deviation.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
 <p>  We expect approximately 3 residential consumers (<m>E(X) = 3.25</m>) to qualify, though this could vary by 1.14 residential consumers.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                   <p>Incorrect. This uses a success probability of <m>p = 0.65</m> rather than the correct <m>p = 0.35</m>. Also, this answer uses the variance, rather than the standard deviation.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                     <p>  We expect maybe 1 residential consumers (<m>E(X) = 0.35</m>) to qualify, give or take 0.48 residential consumers.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This answer gives the mean and standard deviation for a Bernoulli random variable, rather than a Binomial.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>


<exercise label="ex-binomial-drone-thrusters" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Redundant Engineering Systems</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            During a championship sci-fi esports tournament, a media production company deploys an automated broadcast drone array to hover over the arena. To ensure stability, the drone's primary stabilization subsystem features a redundant bank of four identical hover-thrusters. Each individual thruster operates completely independently of the others.
        </p>
        <p>
            Due to the extreme heat during the live event match, each individual thruster has a probability of <m>p = 0.20</m> of overheating and failing before the 1,000-second match countdown timer finishes. Crucially, the stabilization subsystem is designed with fail-safes: it will keep the drone airborne if at least two of the  four thrusters  remain operational.
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part C: Exact Failures (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-exact-failures-input">
<statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that  3 of the 4 thrusters will fail during the match. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to 4 decimal places.
            </p>
            
            <p>
                Probability  = <fillin  answer="0.0256" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback> <p>Correct! Let <m>Y</m> be the number of thrusters that do NOT fail. Then, the answer is:
                   <me>P(Y=1)= \binom{4}{1} (0.80)^1 (0.20)^3</me> </p>
<p>                    
                    Or alternatively, let <m>Y</m> be the number of thrusters that fail. Then, we need:
                    <me>P(Y=3)= \binom{4}{3} (0.20)^3 (0.80)^1</me> </p>
</feedback>

                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
     <!--   <solution>
            <p>
                We are asked to find the probability of experiencing exactly 3 failures across the 4 independent components. In terms of our random variable <m>X</m> (which tracks the number of *surviving* thrusters), this is mathematically equivalent to solving for the point probability of exactly 1 success:
                <me>P(\text{Exactly 3 Fail}) = P(X = 1)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluate this specific coordinate point using the standard Binomial probability mass function formula, where <m>n = 4</m>, <m>k = 1</m>, and the success probability is <m>p = 0.80</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>P(X = 1) \amp = \binom{4}{1} p^1 (1-p)^{n-1}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \binom{4}{1} (0.80)^1 (0.20)^3</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 4 \cdot 0.80 \cdot 0.0080 = 0.0256</mrow>
                </md>
                The exact probability that exactly 3 thrusters fail is precisely <strong>0.0256</strong> (or a 2.56% chance). Notice that this matches the intermediate baseline component we isolated while setting up the complement subtraction loop in Part A!
            </p>
        </solution> -->
    </task>

    <!-- Part A: Probability of Operation (Fill in the Blank) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-survival-probability">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that the drone remains airborne throughout the entire 1,000-second tournament match.
            </p>

            
        </statement>
       <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><m>0.1536</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the probability that exactly two thrusters are working. You need to compute the probability that <em>at least</em> two are working.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p> <m>0.0272</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the probability that the drone does NOT remain airborne, i.e., the probability that  either 0 or 1 thruster continues working. </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p> <m>0.9728</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! To remain airborne: 2, 3, or 4 thrusters must continue to work. We can compute the probability for each of these scenarios using the Binomial probability distribution function and then add them together. </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p> <m>0.8192</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This represents the probability that more than two thrusters remain functional. However, the drone will remain airborne when only two are working. You must include this scenario in your answer. </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p> <m>0.4096</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the probability that all four thrusters remain functional. [It is also the probability that exactly 3 thrusters remain functional.] However, the drone stays airborne as long as at least two thrusters remain operational. You want to find the probability of this occurring.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>

    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Summary Moments & Volatility Analysis (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-moments-mc">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the  expected value and the variance for the number of functioning thrusters (<m>Y</m>). Enter your answers as decimals rounded to 2 decimal places.
            </p>

<p>  <m>E(Y)=</m> <fillin  answer="3.20" width="8" name="e"/> </p>
            <p> <m>V(Y)=</m> <fillin answer="0.64" width="8" name="v"/> </p>
        </statement>

        <evaluation>
            <evaluate submit="e"><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test></evaluate>
             <evaluate submit="v"><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>


    </task>
</exercise>

   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-binom-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>
   



<exercise xml:id="ex-antenna-network-binomial">
    <title> Smart Antenna Connectivity</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A high-performance mobile streaming device utilizes multiple cellular data antennas acting independently of each other. The device succeeds in sending a data packet if any one or more of the antennas successfully establish a connection with the local network hub.  During peak hours, each individual antenna possesses a probability of <m>p = 0.92</m> of successfully establishing a network connection.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose the device has 3 antennas and attempts to send a data packet during peak hours. Let the random variable <m>X_i</m> indicate whether antenna <m>i</m> successfully connects (for <m>i=1,2,3</m>), and let <m>Y = \sum_{i=1}^3 X_i</m> represent the total number of antennas that connect.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    


    <!-- Part 1: Random Variable Identification -->
    <task xml:id="task-network-rv-classification">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What type of random variables are  <m>X_i</m>, <m>i=1,2,3</m>, and  <m>Y</m>? Explain your reasoning.
            </p>
        </statement>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Each individual variable <m>X_i</m> is a <term>Bernoulli random variable</term>. This is because each antenna represents a single isolated trial with exactly two outcomes: success (connecting with probability <m>0.8</m>) or failure (not connecting with probability <m>0.2</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The total sum variable <m>Y</m> is a <term>Binomial random variable</term>. This is because it represents the total number of successes compiled across a fixed number (<m>n = 3</m>) of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) Bernoulli trials.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </solution> -->
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: PMFs and Bar Charts -->
    <task xml:id="task-network-pmfs-and-graphs">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Specify the probability distribution functions for the random variables <m>X_3</m> and <m>Y</m>, plugging in the correct values for the parameter(s). Also sketch graphs (bar charts) of the resulting probability distributions.
            </p>
        </statement>
